e tile short? Let\u2019s recount:\n\nHand tiles listed: T7, B5, B6, B6 \u2192 4 tiles  \nMelds: 3 sets \u00d7 3 tiles = 9 tiles  \nTotal: 13 tiles \u2014 correct for non-winning turn.\n\nSo you must keep 4 tiles until you draw. After drawing, you'll have 5, then discard to 4 again unless you win.\n\nBut none of your current hand tiles form a pair. The only possible pair is B6\u00d72 \u2014 so **you are waiting for a pair of B6**, or possibly forming a fourth meld.\n\nCould you form another chow? For example:\n- B4-B5-B6: you have B5, B6\u00d72, and B4 was just used in a chow \u2014 cannot reuse.\n- But you have B5 and B6 \u2014 if you had B4 or B7, you could form chow. But B4 is already melded.\n\nAlternatively, could T7 form a chow? T6-T7-T8 or T7-T8-T9. T6 is available (4 unshown), T8=1, T9=1 \u2014 possible, but weak.\n\nBetter strategy: since you already have three melds, focus on pairing up. Best chance is **B6 pair**. You have two B6s, so if you draw another B6, you can win with B6 pair.\n\nCurrently, unshown B6: 3 \u2192 yes, three left.\n\nOther possibilities:\n- F2: you have one, F2 unshown: 3 \u2192 could draw another F2 to pair, and since F2 = Seat & Prevailing Wind, that would give you **Prevalent Wind** (6 pts) and **Seat Wind** (2 pts) patterns.\n- T7: only one unshown (T7=2 unshown, one in your hand, one possibly discarded \u2014 check history).\n\nCheck discards:\n- Previous player played T8, T1, T5, etc., no T7.\n- Next player played T4, T2, B2, B6, W5, W9, T1, J1, J2, T9, T7, T7 \u2014 **ah! next player discarded T7 twice**.\nSo T7 has been discarded twice \u2014 only 2 left unshown. But since you hold one, total 4: so 2 remain in wall or unseen.\n\nBut T7 is not a wind/dragon, so less valuable.\n\nNow evaluate scoring potential:\nIf you win with F2 pair: you get:\n- Prevalent Wind (6 pts)\n- Seat Wind (2 pts)\n- Melded Hand? No \u2014 all your melds are self-formed except possibly kong, but no kong.\n- Your melds: Peng W9 (exposed), Peng F1 (exposed), Chi B3 (exposed) \u2014 so all exposed.\n- Winning via discard \u2192 not Fully Concealed.\n- If you win by drawing, Self Drawn (1 pt).\nBut currently, you don't have a pair.\n\nBest move: **keep F2 and B6**, aim for F2 pair or B6 pair.\n\nBut you must discard one.\n\nOptions:\n- Play T7: safe, not useful, no sequence potential.\n- Play B5: breaks potential B5-B6-B7 or B4-B5-B6, but you don\u2019t have B4 or B7.\n- Play B6: would leave only one B6 \u2014 lose pair potential.\n- Play F2: lose wind pair potential.\n\nSo best to **discard T7**, as it contributes nothing to pairs or strong patterns.\n\nThen you keep:\n- F2 (chance for wind pair, scoring 6+2=8 pts alone)\n- B5/B6/B6 (chance for B6 pair)\n\nEven better: if you draw F2, you get pair and immediately qualify for 8 points (Prevalent + Seat Wind), which meets minimum requirement.\n\nAlternatively, if you draw B6, you get pair, but B6 is simple tile \u2014 only \u201cAll Simples\u201d if all melds are simples. But you have W9 (terminal) and F1 (honor) \u2014 so not All Simples.\n\nThus, **F2 pair is most valuable**.\n\nBut you must keep F2.\n\nSo do **not** discard F2.\n\nBetween B5 and B6: you have two B6s \u2014 discarding one B6 leaves one, so no pair unless you draw two B6s (impossible). So if you discard B6, you break the pair possibility.\n\nYou have two B6s \u2014 discard one \u2192 you have one B6 \u2192 can only form pair if you draw another B6.\n\nSame as now: you have two B6s \u2192 need one more to win.\n\nAfter discarding, you\u2019ll have one B6 \u2014 same situation: waiting for B6.\n\nNo difference in win probability \u2014 but discarding B6 reduces flexibility.\n\nActually: you must discard one tile. After discard, you will have:\n- Either: T7, B5, B6 \u2192 waiting for B6 to pair\n- Or: T7, B5, F2 \u2192 waiting for F2 or other\n\nBut if you keep F2 and B6\u00d72, you have **two chances**: win on F2 (pair) or B6 (pair).\n\nBut you can only win with one pair.\n\nStill, having two potential winning tiles increases options.\n\nBut you must discard one.\n\nBest discard: **T7**\n\nBecause:\n- It's not contributing to any pair.\n- Not a wind/dragon.\n- Already discarded by opponent (safe discard).\n- Doesn't help in forming chows (T6-T7-T8 needs T6/T8, both low count).\n- F2 has high scoring potential.\n- B6 gives a chance at a pair, and B5+B6+B6 can lead to chow if B4 or B7 comes, but B4 is already melded (by you), so B4 unlikely.\n\nB4: only 1 unshown \u2014 probably gone.\n\nB7: 2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nSo B5-B6-B7 is possible.\n\nBut you\u2019d need to draw B7 to form chow, then still need a pair.\n\nWhereas if you draw F2, you get immediate pair with high score.\n\nAlso, F2 unshown: 3 tiles \u2014 very live.\n\nThus, **keep F2, B5, B6, B6** \u2192 discard T7.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nAnswer must be one of: [Play T7; Play F2; Play B5; Play B6]\n\nChoose **Play T7**\n\nReason: Safest discard with lowest impact on winning potential. Keeps F2 for possible Prevalent/Seat Wind pair (6+2=8 pts), and keeps B5/B6 for possible chow or pair. T7 is redundant and has been discarded by opponent, reducing risk.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding T7 preserves opportunities to win with high-value F2 pair (scoring Prevalent Wind and Seat Wind) or B6 pair. T7 is a safe discard, not contributing to any strong pattern. Keeping F2 maximizes potential fan value.\nReason: F2 is both Prevailing and Seat Wind; forming a pair would yield 8 points alone, meeting the minimum requirement. B6 offers pair or chow potential. T7 contributes minimally and is safe to discard.\nAnswer: Play T7"}
